{
  "id": "hyrq6e2",
  "title": "Chapter 10: Acids, Bases and Salts",
  "order": 59,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "4m8ogrt",
      "content": "Acids, bases and salts are important classes of chemical compounds that play a crucial role in chemistry and daily life. These substances participate in numerous chemical reactions and are widely used in industries, laboratories and households."
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "pH Range",
          "value": "0 - 14",
          "icon": "info"
        },
        {
          "label": "Neutral pH",
          "value": "7.0",
          "icon": "check"
        },
        {
          "label": "Acidic pH",
          "value": "< 7",
          "icon": "book"
        },
        {
          "label": "Basic pH",
          "value": "> 7",
          "icon": "award"
        }
      ],
      "id": "i3s6yy8"
    },
    {
      "id": "21zrmeb",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Acids"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Acids release hydrogen ions (H+) in aqueous solutions and turn blue litmus red.",
        "Bases release hydroxide ions (OH-) in aqueous solutions and turn red litmus blue.",
        "Alkalis are bases that dissolve in water, such as Sodium Hydroxide (NaOH) and Potassium Hydroxide (KOH).",
        "The pH scale quantifies hydrogen ion concentration, ranging from 0 (highly acidic) to 14 (highly alkaline).",
        "Neutralization is an exothermic reaction between an acid and a base that produces salt and water."
      ],
      "id": "m30v64b"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2cbzpyq",
      "content": "• Acids are substances that produce hydrogen ions (H⁺) when dissolved in water."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "q4ranwr",
      "content": "• They have a sour taste."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "dxzax0z",
      "content": "• They turn blue litmus paper red."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "b9gdruq",
      "content": "• They react with metals to produce hydrogen gas."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "l971fpe",
      "content": "Examples of common acids:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "01ka1h3",
      "content": "• Hydrochloric acid (HCl)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "nz0ncfy",
      "content": "• Sulphuric acid (H₂SO₄)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "y07ujnn",
      "content": "• Nitric acid (HNO₃)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "v2dvojc",
      "content": "• Acetic acid (CH₃COOH)"
    },
    {
      "id": "h1dmzsb",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Bases"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "s2nn71b",
      "content": "• Bases are substances that produce hydroxide ions (OH⁻) in aqueous solution."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "x8ohaaj",
      "content": "• They taste bitter and feel slippery."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "y8uzvkh",
      "content": "• They turn red litmus paper blue."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "q7d5vgh",
      "content": "Examples of bases:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "i2tnonp",
      "content": "• Sodium hydroxide (NaOH)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "0o8vag0",
      "content": "• Potassium hydroxide (KOH)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "lsbw3o0",
      "content": "• Calcium hydroxide Ca(OH)₂"
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "Neutralization reaction is a chemical reaction in which an acid and an alkali react to form salt and water.",
      "id": "obs1hmb"
    },
    {
      "id": "nooxnb2",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Alkalis"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "l6errjg",
      "content": "Alkalis are bases that dissolve in water and produce hydroxide ions. All alkalis are bases but all bases are not alkalis."
    },
    {
      "id": "872v4oc",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Properties of Acids and Bases"
    },
